    "description": "The same five runs as v2_data-protect_runs_summary, in the run-LIST shape: facts under localBackupInfo, bytes under localSnapshotStats, times spelled runStartTimeUsecs. Serves the two fallbacks the client reaches for when runs/summary times out, and - on the kVMware runs only - the objects[] array that includeObjectDetails=true adds, which is what the v0.1.8 protected-object probe reads.",
    "source": "https://developers.cohesity.com/v1-cluster-7.4/reference/getprotectiongroupruns.md",
    "capturedAt": null,
    "clusterVersion": "7.4",
    "warning": "SYNTHETIC, and thinner evidence than most. /v2/data-protect/protection-runs is not in the 7.3.2 reference this extension was written from at all - only the per-group /protection-groups/{id}/runs is - so this body is the per-group response shape used for both, which is exactly why domain.parse_run_list accepts either spelling of every field rather than trusting this file. Run ids MATCH the runs/summary fixture on purpose: the same run reported by two endpoints must be counted once, and that is only demonstrable if the ids agree. The objects[] arrays are the most invented part of this file: objects[].object is modelled straight from GetProtectionGroupRuns' ProtectionRunObject schema and NOBODY HAS SEEN IT ON A CLUSTER. That is precisely why the probe exists - do not read the uuid values here as evidence that a real cluster publishes BIOS UUIDs. The names are obvious placeholders so that a test asserting no object name ever reaches a diagnostic is asserting something."
